    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This used to be an easy argument, and it is not any more. Water-based deck stains have closed most of the historic gap, and the honest comparison now turns on two or three specific properties rather than a general verdict about which is better.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose <strong>oil-based</strong> if you value deep penetration, a rich look on the grain, and easy recoating — it wears away gradually, so future maintenance is usually a wash and a fresh coat rather than a strip. It suits cedar, redwood and older weathered decks particularly well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose <strong>water-based</strong> if you value colour retention, mildew resistance, fast drying and easy cleanup. It holds its pigment longer under sun, does not feed mildew the way natural oils can, and is far more pleasant to work with in every practical respect.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Distinction That Matters Most</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Oil is easier to live with over ten years. Water-based looks better for longer between coats.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you dread the maintenance, oil's graceful failure mode is worth more than a season of extra colour. If you want the deck to look freshly stained as long as possible and do not mind a harder reset eventually, water-based earns its place.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-they-cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Each One Actually Cures
          </h2>

          <p>
            An oil-based stain carries pigment and resin in a drying oil, usually linseed, tung, or a modified alkyd. It soaks into the wood, then the solvent evaporates and the oil cures by oxidation — reacting with oxygen from the air and hardening over days. That process is slow, temperature-dependent, and the reason oil stains stay workable for a long time on the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            A water-based stain carries acrylic or modified-acrylic resin particles suspended in water. As the water evaporates the particles pack together and coalesce into a continuous film. That happens quickly, which is why the stain is dry in an hour, and it only happens properly above the minimum film-forming temperature, which is why cold-weather application ruins a water-based finish more decisively than an oil one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those two mechanisms explain nearly every practical difference below. Oxidation gives penetration, richness, long working time and graceful wear. Coalescence gives speed, colour stability, and a finish that behaves a little more like a film. Modern hybrids blur the line, using water as a carrier for an oil-modified resin, and they genuinely do capture some of both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Side by Side
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Property</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Oil-Based</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Water-Based</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deeper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shallower, more surface</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Colour retention</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fades and can darken</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Holds colour longer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mildew resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Weaker — oils feed mildew</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Better</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dry to touch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several hours or more</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 1–2 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Working time</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long, forgiving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short, lap marks come easily</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Recoating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually clean and recoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">More likely to need stripping</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cleanup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mineral spirits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soap and water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Odour and VOCs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher; restricted in some states</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read that table as a set of trade-offs rather than a scorecard, because the rows are not equally important to everyone. For a shaded, damp deck the mildew row outweighs everything else. For someone who resents deck maintenance, the recoating row is the only one that matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penetration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Penetration, and Why Species Changes the Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil penetrates further into wood than water-based resin does, and on absorbent softwoods that difference is visible. The stain sits inside the fibres rather than near the surface, so it resists abrasion better, moves with the wood, and wears thin gradually rather than lifting at an edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            That advantage is largest on cedar and redwood, which are soft, porous and full of natural extractives that oil complements. It is real but smaller on pressure-treated southern yellow pine, which takes both types well once dry. It largely disappears on dense tropical hardwoods, which barely absorb anything and are usually finished with specialised penetrating oils rather than either conventional product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based stains also raise the grain, because water swells wood fibres. On a smooth deck that can mean a slightly rougher surface after the first coat, which is why some people sand lightly between coats on railings where the feel matters. Our guides to <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-cedar/">cedar stains</a> and <a href="/best-deck-stain-for-pressure-treated-wood/">pressure-treated stains</a> cover the species-specific choices in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="colour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Colour Retention and Mildew
          </h2>

          <p>
            Acrylic resins are more ultraviolet-stable than drying oils, which is the reason water-based stains generally hold their colour longer. An oil-based finish fades under sun and, in some formulations, ambers or darkens over time as the oil itself yellows — an effect that is charming on some woods and unwelcome on others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mildew runs the other way, and the mechanism is worth understanding. Natural drying oils are organic material, and mildew will happily feed on them. Both types include mildewcides, but a water-based acrylic does not offer the same food source, so on shaded, humid or north-facing decks water-based products stay visually cleaner for longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your deck is in deep shade, under trees, or in a humid climate, treat that as a strong argument for water-based. If it is in full sun and you care most about how the grain looks, oil still has a genuine aesthetic advantage that many people prefer. Neither preference is wrong; they are answers to different decks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Application and Cleanup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil is more forgiving to apply. Its long open time means the wet edge stays workable, lap marks are harder to create, and you can go back over an area minutes later to even it out. That matters on a big deck, on a hot day, or for anyone doing this for the first time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based stain demands more discipline. It sets quickly, so you must keep moving, maintain a wet edge, and never stop mid-board. On a hot, breezy day the working time can shrink to almost nothing, which is why shade discipline matters even more with these products. The payoff is that the deck is usable far sooner and much less vulnerable to an unexpected shower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cleanup is not a small consideration. Oil means mineral spirits, careful rag disposal, and the genuine fire risk of oil-soaked rags, which generate heat as they cure and should be spread flat to dry or stored in water in a sealed metal container. Water-based means rinsing brushes in the sink. Over a long weekend that difference is more significant than it sounds. Whichever you choose, the technique is the same and is covered in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Oil-Soaked Rags Are a Real Fire Hazard</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">Drying oils generate heat as they oxidise, and a bunched-up pile of oily rags can reach ignition temperature on its own. Spread them flat outdoors to dry completely, or submerge them in water in a sealed metal container, before disposal. Never leave them in a heap in a bin or a garage.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="switching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Switching Between Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where the decision becomes long-term rather than one-off. Applying a water-based stain over an oil-based finish that is still present is unreliable: the acrylic cannot bond well to an oily surface, and the result is often a coating that lifts within a season. The reverse — oil over water-based — is somewhat more tolerant but still not recommended by most manufacturers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The test is the same one that governs everything else. If the old finish has genuinely worn away and water soaks into the boards everywhere, the wood is bare enough that either type can go on. If water beads anywhere, the old finish is still there and switching types means stripping first, which is covered in <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck stain</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practically, that means the first choice sets a default for years. Picking oil commits you to oil-friendly recoats unless you are willing to strip; picking water-based does the same in reverse. It is worth deciding deliberately rather than by whichever can was on the shelf, and it is another reason to read the recoat question in <a href="/can-you-stain-over-old-deck-stain/">staining over old deck stain</a> before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="voc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. VOC Rules and What Is Actually Available
          </h2>

          <p>
            Volatile organic compound regulations vary by state and region, and they have reshaped this category. Traditional high-solvent oil stains are restricted or unavailable in some areas, and manufacturers have responded with lower-VOC oil formulations, waterborne alkyds, and hybrid products that carry an oil-modified resin in a water base.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical consequence is that the same brand and product name can be a different formulation depending on where you buy it, and reviews written elsewhere may not describe the can on your local shelf. If a product performed differently than you expected based on a recommendation, a regional formulation difference is a plausible explanation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrids are worth a serious look. They apply and clean up much like a water-based product while penetrating better than a straight acrylic, and they sidestep some of the solvent restrictions. They are not a perfect substitute for a traditional oil on cedar, but the gap has narrowed considerably. Our <a href="/best-oil-based-deck-stain/">oil-based stain testing</a> and <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">general deck stain reviews</a> cover how specific products behave in practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Which One for Which Deck</h2>
        <p>
          For a shaded, damp, north-facing deck under trees, water-based is the better default. Mildew is the dominant failure mode in that situation, and the acrylic chemistry does not feed it the way natural oils do. The colour holds well in low light, and the faster dry time is genuinely useful when the deck rarely gets a long dry spell.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a weathered cedar or redwood deck in the sun, oil generally looks better and behaves better. It penetrates the soft, thirsty fibres, brings out the warmth of the grain in a way acrylics struggle to match, and wears away evenly so the next recoat is a wash rather than a strip. Accept that the colour will shift over the years and that mildewcide performance matters more here.
        </p>
        <p>
          For an ordinary exposed pressure-treated deck — the most common case by far — either works well, and the deciding factor should be your own priorities. If you want the least painful maintenance over a decade, choose oil. If you want the deck to look freshly done for as long as possible each cycle, choose water-based. If you cannot decide, a modern hybrid splits the difference honestly.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Cost, Coverage and What You Actually Spend</h2>
        <p>
          Price per gallon is broadly comparable between the two types at similar quality levels, and neither is reliably cheaper. What differs more is coverage, which tends to be lower for oil on absorbent wood precisely because it penetrates — thirsty cedar can drink significantly more of an oil stain than of a water-based one, so budget the pessimistic end of the coverage range.
        </p>
        <p>
          Consumables tilt the other way. Oil work needs mineral spirits for cleanup, and brushes and rollers used with oil are more likely to be thrown away than cleaned properly, whereas water-based tools rinse out and last for years. Over several deck jobs that adds up, and it is a small but real running cost that never appears in the shelf price comparison.
        </p>
        <p>
          The larger cost is the eventual reset. A deck maintained with penetrating oil stains may go a decade or more without ever needing stripping, because each recoat is a wash and a coat. A deck maintained with film-forming water-based products is more likely to need a strip at some point, and that is a weekend of caustic chemistry plus the product to do it. Factor that into any ten-year comparison.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Testing Before You Commit</h2>
        <p>
          Whichever direction you lean, buy a quart before you buy five gallons and test it on the actual deck. Stain colour and behaviour depend heavily on the species, age and absorbency of the wood, and the same product genuinely looks different on new treated pine, weathered cedar and a stripped and sanded board.
        </p>
        <p>
          Test somewhere inconspicuous but representative — under a step, at the edge of a section that will be covered by furniture, or on an offcut of the same lumber left over from construction. Let it dry fully and look at it in daylight, ideally after a day or two, because both types shift as they cure and an oil stain in particular can darken noticeably.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use the test patch to check absorption as well as colour. How fast the stain disappears tells you whether the deck is a one-coat or a two-coat job, which affects how much you buy, and it confirms that the preparation actually worked. Our guides to <a href="/how-many-coats-of-deck-stain/">how many coats</a> and <a href="/deck-stain-colors-guide/">deck stain colours</a> cover reading those results.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting Water-Based Over Existing Oil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic does not bond well to an oily surface, and the new coat often lifts within a season. If water still beads anywhere on the deck, the old oil finish is present and switching types means stripping first. The water test is the only reliable check.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Water-Based Stain in the Cold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acrylic resins need to be above their minimum film-forming temperature to coalesce into a continuous film. Below it, the coating never properly forms and wears away within a season. The overnight low matters as much as the daytime high.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Water-Based Like Oil on the Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil's long open time forgives stopping and going back over an area; water-based sets fast and punishes both. Keep a wet edge, work board by board without pausing, and stay out of direct sun where the working time collapses to almost nothing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Oily Rags in a Pile</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drying oils generate heat as they cure, and a heap of oil-soaked rags can self-ignite. Spread them flat outdoors until fully dry, or submerge them in water in a sealed metal container. This is the one genuinely dangerous part of an otherwise safe job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Oil for a Shaded, Damp Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural oils are food for mildew, so an oil-stained deck under trees or in a humid climate tends to develop black spotting sooner. Where mildew is the dominant problem rather than sun, a water-based product is the better-matched chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based or water-based deck stain better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither universally. Oil penetrates deeper, looks richer on the grain and recoats easily because it wears rather than peels. Water-based holds colour longer, resists mildew better, dries faster and cleans up with water. Choose oil for easy maintenance, water-based for colour retention.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put water-based stain over oil-based stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. Water-based products applied over an oil finish that is still present often fail to bond and peel. If the oil stain has worn away completely and the wood accepts water everywhere, a water-based product can go on; otherwise strip first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does oil-based deck stain last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily longer in years, but it fails more gracefully. Oil wears away gradually, so a recoat is usually a clean and a fresh coat. Water-based often holds colour longer but is more likely to form a film that eventually needs stripping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which deck stain resists mildew better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-based generally does. The natural oils in an oil-based product are themselves a food source for mildew, which is why oil-stained decks in shaded or humid spots develop black spotting sooner. Both carry mildewcides, but the acrylic starts from a better position.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based deck stain being phased out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In some regions, yes. Volatile organic compound rules have restricted high-solvent formulations, so availability varies by state and the same brand name can be a different formula in different places. Many manufacturers now offer lower-VOC oil or hybrid versions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which dries faster, oil or water-based deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-based, by a wide margin — typically dry to the touch in an hour or two against several hours for oil, and fully cured in days rather than a week or more. That is an advantage against rain and a disadvantage for working time on a hot day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better for cedar, oil or water-based stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oil generally looks better on cedar and redwood, penetrating the soft absorbent fibres well and enhancing the natural warmth of the grain. Water-based products raise the grain more and, on tannin-rich species, sometimes need extra care to avoid discolouration.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you mix oil and water-based deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. They are chemically incompatible and will not combine into a usable product. Even on the deck, applying one over the other without fully removing the first is unreliable, which is why switching types usually means stripping back to bare wood.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

