        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Oil penetrates deeper, looks richer on cedar and redwood, gives a long forgiving working time, and wears away gracefully so future recoats are a wash and a coat. Water-based holds its colour longer, resists mildew better, dries in an hour, and cleans up in the sink.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Match the chemistry to the deck's dominant problem. Shade, damp and mildew point to water-based. Sun, absorbent softwood and a dislike of maintenance point to oil. Modern hybrids genuinely split the difference and are worth considering when neither case is clear-cut.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you pick, understand that you are setting a default for years, because switching types later usually means stripping back to bare wood. Test a quart on the actual deck before committing to five gallons, and check the water test before assuming you can change direction.
          </p>
